  "notes": "The early jointly led programme and later peaceful economic strategy are distinguished; conflicting positions remain mixed. Soviet coercion is analysed with explicit moral reservations. Descriptions of fascism and feudal sovereignty are not endorsements. Economic limits to growth do not establish environmentalist policy. Sacred experience does not imply clerical government, and fictional sexual violence does not establish criminal-law positions. All statements were reviewed; no historical inferences were required.\n\nMerged with an independent claude-sonnet-5-5 audit: 11 answers taken from it, 12 agreed, 0 conflicts settled by the integrator.\n\nclaude-sonnet-5-5 notes: Independent second pass. Searched his own texts: Contre-Attaque manifesto and Cahiers (1935-36), Visions of Excess (Notion of Expenditure, Psychological Structure of Fascism, Popular Front in the Street, Sacred Conspiracy, Nietzsche and the Fascists, Propositions, Use Value of Sade), The Accursed Share vol. I (full text) and vols. II-III (skimmed; the scan is garbled, so none of it is cited). Not obtained: Eroticism, Literature and Evil, Inner Experience, On Nietzsche and the Oeuvres completes (lending-only or unavailable). Government, world and liberty themes are thin because he wrote little on institutions or foreign policy beyond 1935-36 and the Marshall Plan chapter. His views shifted from armed revolution (1935-36) to peaceful evolution (1949); answers use the later statement where it clearly addresses the question. The Psychological Structure of Fascism and Propositions describe fascism and sovereignty analytically and are not used as endorsements.",
